---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SEO from '../../components/SEO.astro';

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "The Complete Portable AC Buying Guide",
    "description": "Everything apartment renters need to know before buying a portable air conditioner. Single-hose vs dual-hose, SACC BTU ratings, electrical requirements, drainage, and window venting.",
    "author": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "Cooling Support"
    },
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "Cooling Support",
      "url": "https://coolingsupport.com"
    },
    "mainEntityOfPage": {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "@id": "https://coolingsupport.com/portable-ac/buying-guide"
    }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I buy a single-hose or dual-hose portable AC?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Dual-hose is almost always the better choice. Single-hose units create negative air pressure in your room, pulling hot outside air in through gaps and reducing efficiency by 20-40%. Dual-hose models maintain neutral pressure and cool rooms significantly faster. The extra $100-$300 is worth it if the portable AC is your primary cooling source."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between ASHRAE and DOE SACC BTU ratings?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "ASHRAE is the old rating system that only measured the cooling output of the compressor. DOE SACC (Seasonally Adjusted Cooling Capacity) is the newer, more accurate standard that accounts for heat generated by the unit itself and heat drawn into the room through negative pressure. A unit rated at 14,000 BTU ASHRAE is typically around 10,000 BTU SACC. Always shop by the SACC number."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can a portable AC trip my breaker?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Most portable ACs draw 9-13 amps, and most bedroom circuits are only 15 amps shared with other outlets. If you're also running a TV, lamp, fan, or computer on the same circuit, you can easily exceed the breaker's limit. Never use an extension cord with a portable AC — always plug directly into a wall outlet."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
];
---

<Layout title="Best Portable Air Conditioner Reddit Buying Guide — Portable AC Unit vs Window Unit">
  <SEO
    slot="head"
    title="Best Portable Air Conditioner Reddit Buying Guide | Portable AC Unit vs Window Unit"
    description="Everything you need to know to find the best portable air conditioner (Reddit-approved). Compares portable AC units vs window units. Covers dual-hose, SACC BTU ratings, and more."
    type="article"
    schema={schema}
  />

  <!-- ===== DARK HERO ===== -->
  <div class="relative bg-slate-900 pt-16 pb-24 border-b border-slate-800 text-center overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute top-0 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[1000px] h-[350px] opacity-20 pointer-events-none">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-cyan-500 via-blue-600 to-transparent blur-[120px] rounded-full"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 relative z-10">
      <nav class="flex justify-center items-center gap-2 text-xs text-slate-400 mb-8 font-medium">
        <a class="hover:text-blue-400 transition-colors cursor-pointer" href="/">Home</a>
        <svg class="w-3 h-3 text-slate-650" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        <a class="hover:text-blue-400 transition-colors cursor-pointer" href="/portable-ac/"">Portable AC Hub</a>
        <svg class="w-3 h-3 text-slate-650" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        <span class="text-slate-300">Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>

      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/25 text-blue-300 text-xs font-bold px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full mb-5 tracking-widest uppercase font-mono">
        <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-blue-400" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 6.253v13m0-13C10.832 5.477 9.246 5 7.5 5S4.168 5.477 3 6.253v13C4.168 18.477 5.754 18 7.5 18s3.332.477 4.5 1.253m0-13C13.168 5.477 14.754 5 16.5 5c1.747 0 3.332.477 4.5 1.253v13C19.832 18.477 18.247 18 16.5 18c-1.746 0-3.332.477-4.5 1.253"/></svg>
        Apartment Cooling Guide
      </div>

      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight leading-tight mb-4 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        Best Portable Air Conditioner <span class="text-blue-300">Reddit</span> Buying Guide
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-slate-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed font-light mb-4">
        Everything you need to know to find the best portable air conditioner (Reddit-approved). We compare portable AC units vs window units, explain single vs dual-hose, and help you find the right SACC BTU rating.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 font-medium">
        10 minute read &middot; Last updated June 2026
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===== CONTENT SECTIONS ===== -->
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 space-y-10">

    <!-- ===== SECTION 1: Do You Actually Need a Portable AC? ===== -->
    <section class="bg-white rounded-3xl shadow-2xl shadow-slate-200/50 border border-slate-100 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-8 md:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-blue-100 text-blue-600">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8.228 9c.549-1.165 2.03-2 3.772-2 2.21 0 4 1.343 4 3 0 1.4-1.278 2.575-3.006 2.907-.542.104-.994.54-.994 1.093m0 3h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">Do You Actually Need a Portable AC?</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Portable air conditioners are <strong>not the most efficient</strong> way to cool a room — that's just the reality. A window AC of the same BTU rating will outperform a portable unit every time. So why do they exist? Because sometimes a portable AC is your <strong>only option</strong>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">When a portable AC makes sense:</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 ml-1">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
              <span><strong>Your apartment lease prohibits window units</strong> — many buildings ban them due to falling risks or aesthetic rules.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
              <span><strong>Your HOA won't allow exterior modifications</strong> — no mini-splits, no window units, no through-wall installs.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
              <span><strong>Your windows don't accommodate standard AC units</strong> — casement windows, sliding doors, or unusually shaped openings.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
              <span><strong>You're renting and don't want a permanent install</strong> — portable ACs pack up and move with you.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
              <span><strong>You need to cool a room without window access</strong> — server rooms, interior offices, or basements (with nearby venting).</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mt-4">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-600 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-2.5L13.732 4c-.77-.833-1.964-.833-2.732 0L4.082 16.5c-.77.833.192 2.5 1.732 2.5z"/></svg>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-amber-800 mb-1">When NOT to buy a portable AC</p>
                <p class="text-amber-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  If you <em>can</em> install a window air conditioner, <strong>always choose the window unit</strong>. A 8,000 BTU window AC will cool a room better than a 14,000 BTU (ASHRAE) portable unit, cost less to buy, and use significantly less electricity. Window ACs don't create negative air pressure and waste zero cooled air exhausting heat. Portable ACs are a last resort, not a first choice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== SECTION 2: Single-Hose vs Dual-Hose ===== -->
    <section class="bg-white rounded-3xl shadow-2xl shadow-slate-200/50 border border-slate-100 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-8 md:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-cyan-100 text-cyan-600">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8 7h12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l-4 4m0 6H4m0 0l4 4m-4-4l4-4"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">Single-Hose vs Dual-Hose: The Most Important Decision</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            This is the single most impactful decision you'll make. The difference isn't just about specifications — it fundamentally changes how the unit interacts with your room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">How a single-hose unit works:</h3>
          <p>
            A single-hose portable AC pulls air <strong>from inside your room</strong>, runs it over the condenser to absorb heat, then exhausts that hot air outside through the hose. The problem? It just removed air from your room. That creates <strong>negative air pressure</strong>, which forces hot outside air to rush in through every crack — under doors, around outlets, through gaps in the window kit. You're literally fighting against yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">How a dual-hose unit works:</h3>
          <p>
            A dual-hose unit uses a <strong>separate intake hose</strong> to pull outside air for cooling the condenser, and a second hose to exhaust the heat. Your room's air stays in your room. No negative pressure, no hot air infiltration, and significantly faster cooling.
          </p>

          <!-- Comparison Table -->
          <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold rounded-tl-xl">Feature</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 text-center font-bold">Single-Hose</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 text-center font-bold rounded-tr-xl">Dual-Hose</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-slate-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Price Range</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center">$250 – $400</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center">$400 – $700</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Efficiency</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center">
                    <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-amber-600 font-medium">
                      <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3"/></svg>
                      Lower (negative pressure)
                    </span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center">
                    <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-emerald-600 font-medium">
                      <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 10l7-7m0 0l7 7m-7-7v18"/></svg>
                      Higher (neutral pressure)
                    </span>
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Cooling Speed</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center text-amber-600 font-medium">Slower</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center text-emerald-600 font-medium">Faster</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Best For</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center">Small rooms, occasional use</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center">Primary cooling, bedrooms</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Noise Level</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center text-slate-500">Similar</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center text-slate-500">Similar</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <!-- Verdict -->
          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mt-4">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-blue-600 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-blue-800 mb-1">Our Verdict</p>
                <p class="text-blue-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  <strong>Dual-hose is worth the extra money</strong> if this is your main cooling source. You'll cool faster, use less energy over the summer, and actually reach your target temperature instead of running the unit all day. The only exception: if you're cooling a tiny room (&lt;150 sq ft) only a few times per summer, a single-hose will do the job.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== SECTION 3: Understanding BTU Ratings ===== -->
    <section class="bg-white rounded-3xl shadow-2xl shadow-slate-200/50 border border-slate-100 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-8 md:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-indigo-100 text-indigo-600">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 7h6m0 10v-3m-3 3h.01M9 17h.01M9 14h.01M12 14h.01M15 11h.01M12 11h.01M9 11h.01M7 21h10a2 2 0 002-2V5a2 2 0 00-2-2H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v14a2 2 0 002 2z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">Understanding BTU Ratings: ASHRAE vs DOE SACC</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            This is where portable AC shopping gets confusing. There are <strong>two completely different BTU rating systems</strong> in use right now, and manufacturers love to use whichever makes their unit look more powerful.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">The old system: ASHRAE</h3>
          <p>
            The ASHRAE rating measured only the raw cooling output of the compressor in a lab — essentially, how cold the air coming out of the vent was. It completely ignored the heat the unit generates, the hot exhaust air, and the negative pressure effect. A unit rated at 14,000 BTU by ASHRAE sounded great, but the <em>real-world</em> cooling capacity was far less.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">The new system: DOE SACC</h3>
          <p>
            The Department of Energy introduced the <strong>Seasonally Adjusted Cooling Capacity (SACC)</strong> rating to fix this. SACC accounts for the heat the unit adds to the room, the infiltration caused by negative pressure (in single-hose units), and seasonal temperature variations. It's a much more honest number.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-3">Conversion Guide</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-3">Here's how the old ASHRAE numbers translate to real SACC performance:</p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3">
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-4 text-center border border-slate-100">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Old ASHRAE</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-slate-400 line-through">14,000</p>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-slate-300 mx-auto my-1" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3"/></svg>
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">New SACC</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-blue-600">10,000</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-4 text-center border border-slate-100">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Old ASHRAE</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-slate-400 line-through">12,000</p>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-slate-300 mx-auto my-1" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3"/></svg>
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">New SACC</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-blue-600">8,000</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-4 text-center border border-slate-100">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Old ASHRAE</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-slate-400 line-through">10,000</p>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-slate-300 mx-auto my-1" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3"/></svg>
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">New SACC</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-blue-600">7,000</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-4 text-center border border-slate-100">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Old ASHRAE</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-slate-400 line-through">8,000</p>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-slate-300 mx-auto my-1" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3"/></svg>
              <p class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">New SACC</p>
              <p class="text-xl font-extrabold text-blue-600">5,500</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mt-4">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-emerald-800 mb-1">The Golden Rule</p>
                <p class="text-emerald-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  <strong>Always shop by the SACC number.</strong> If a listing only shows the ASHRAE rating (the bigger number), be skeptical — the manufacturer may be trying to make the unit look more powerful than it really is. Look for the DOE SACC rating on the EnergyGuide label.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== SECTION 4: Electrical ===== -->
    <section class="bg-white rounded-3xl shadow-2xl shadow-slate-200/50 border border-slate-100 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-8 md:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-amber-100 text-amber-600">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">Electrical: Will It Blow My Fuse?</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            This is the question nobody asks until the breaker trips at 2 AM. Portable air conditioners are <strong>power-hungry appliances</strong>, and most apartment circuits weren't designed with them in mind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">The numbers you need to know:</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 ml-1">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 font-extrabold text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</span>
              <span><strong>Most bedroom circuits are 15 amps, shared</strong> across multiple outlets. That ceiling light, your phone charger, your TV — they're all on the same circuit as the outlet you're plugging the AC into.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 font-extrabold text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</span>
              <span><strong>Portable ACs draw 9 – 13 amps</strong> depending on the size. A 14,000 BTU ASHRAE unit can pull 12+ amps on its own. That leaves you with just 3 amps for everything else.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 font-extrabold text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</span>
              <span><strong>Compressor startup surges</strong> draw even more current momentarily. Even if you're right at the limit, that startup spike can push you over.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mt-4">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-red-600 mt-0.5 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-2.5L13.732 4c-.77-.833-1.964-.833-2.732 0L4.082 16.5c-.77.833.192 2.5 1.732 2.5z"/></svg>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-1">Never use an extension cord</p>
                <p class="text-red-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  Extension cords create resistance, which generates heat. At 10+ amps, that heat can melt the cord's insulation and start a fire. <strong>Always plug your portable AC directly into a wall outlet.</strong> This isn't a recommendation — it's a safety requirement listed in every portable AC manual.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-5 mt-4 border border-slate-200 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">Not sure if your circuit can handle it?</p>
            <a href="/portable-ac/breaker-check/"" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-amber-500 hover:bg-amber-600 text-white font-bold px-6 py-3 rounded-xl transition-all shadow-lg shadow-amber-500/25">
              <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg>
              Use our Breaker Check Calculator
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== SECTION 5: The Water Problem ===== -->
    <section class="bg-white rounded-3xl shadow-2xl shadow-slate-200/50 border border-slate-100 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-8 md:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-sky-100 text-sky-600">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19.428 15.428a2 2 0 00-1.022-.547l-2.387-.477a6 6 0 00-3.86.517l-.318.158a6 6 0 01-3.86.517L6.05 15.21a2 2 0 00-1.806.547M8 4h8l-1 1v5.172a2 2 0 00.586 1.414l5 5c1.26 1.26.367 3.414-1.415 3.414H4.828c-1.782 0-2.674-2.154-1.414-3.414l5-5A2 2 0 009 10.172V5L8 4z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">The Water Problem: Drainage & Humidity</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Air conditioning is fundamentally a <strong>dehumidification process</strong>. When warm, humid air passes over the cold evaporator coils, moisture condenses out — just like water droplets on a cold glass. That water has to go somewhere.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">Auto-Evaporative Models</h3>
          <p>
            Most modern portable ACs are <strong>auto-evaporative</strong> — they recirculate the condensed water back over the condenser coils and exhaust it as vapor through the hot air hose. In moderate humidity (below ~60%), you may never need to drain the unit at all. But in high-humidity climates like Florida, Houston, or the mid-Atlantic, the system can't evaporate water as fast as it collects it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">Manual Drain / Bucket Emptying</h3>
          <p>
            When humidity overwhelms the auto-evaporative system, water fills the internal reservoir and the unit shuts off with an error code (usually <strong>E1</strong>, <strong>FL</strong>, or <strong>P1</strong>). You then have to physically drain it. In the worst conditions, this can happen <strong>every 2-4 hours</strong> — including the middle of the night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">Setting Up a Gravity Drain</h3>
          <p>
            The permanent solution is a <strong>continuous gravity drain</strong>. Most portable ACs have a drain port at the bottom or back. Attach a garden hose adapter, run a hose to a floor drain, bathtub, or bucket, and let gravity do the work. The key requirement: the hose must slope <em>downward</em> the entire way. No uphill sections, no kinks, no dips.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-sky-50 rounded-2xl p-5 mt-4 border border-sky-200 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-3">How often will you need to empty the bucket?</p>
            <a href="/portable-ac/bucket-fill-time/"" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-sky-500 hover:bg-sky-600 text-white font-bold px-6 py-3 rounded-xl transition-all shadow-lg shadow-sky-500/25">
              <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19.428 15.428a2 2 0 00-1.022-.547l-2.387-.477a6 6 0 00-3.86.517l-.318.158a6 6 0 01-3.86.517L6.05 15.21a2 2 0 00-1.806.547M8 4h8l-1 1v5.172a2 2 0 00.586 1.414l5 5c1.26 1.26.367 3.414-1.415 3.414H4.828c-1.782 0-2.674-2.154-1.414-3.414l5-5A2 2 0 009 10.172V5L8 4z"/></svg>
              Use our Bucket Fill Time Estimator
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== SECTION 6: Window Venting ===== -->
    <section class="bg-white rounded-3xl shadow-2xl shadow-slate-200/50 border border-slate-100 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-8 md:p-10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
