            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M4 5a1 1 0 011-1h14a1 1 0 011 1v2a1 1 0 01-1 1H5a1 1 0 01-1-1V5zm0 8a1 1 0 011-1h6a1 1 0 011 1v6a1 1 0 01-1 1H5a1 1 0 01-1-1v-6zm12 0a1 1 0 011-1h2a1 1 0 011 1v6a1 1 0 01-1 1h-2a1 1 0 01-1-1v-6z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">Window Venting: Making the Kit Fit</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Every portable AC needs to vent hot exhaust air outside. Most units come with a <strong>slider window kit</strong> — a flat plastic panel that sits in a horizontal or vertical slider window opening. But not every window is standard, and the included kits are often flimsy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">Standard slider kits</h3>
          <p>
            The included kit is designed for <strong>single- or double-hung windows</strong> that slide up and down. You open the window, place the panel in the gap, connect the exhaust hose, and close the window against the panel. Most kits adjust from about 20" to 48" wide. If your window opening falls in that range, you're set.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">When you need to cut or extend</h3>
          <p>
            If your window is wider than 48", you'll need to <strong>extend the kit</strong> with a piece of plexiglass, foam board, or a second kit. If your window is a casement (crank-out) or sliding glass door, you'll need a different approach entirely — usually a custom-cut plexiglass panel or a casement-specific adapter kit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">Sealing gaps</h3>
          <p>
            The biggest mistake people make is leaving gaps around the venting panel. <strong>Every gap lets hot air back in</strong> and lets cooled air escape. Use weatherstripping foam tape around the edges of the panel and where the window frame meets the panel. For the exhaust hose connection, wrap the joint with foil HVAC tape — not duct tape, which deteriorates in heat.
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-4">
